        <p>public bodies in some parts of the country are slow to embark, yet they are public conveniences of the most important kind.</p>
        <p>The Common- Tm he Workmen's Copensation non . Bill is still the moat important measure Hill before Parliament, and it is gradu- ally making headway thump!, the House of Common,. In PlOlllO respects it is being improved by amendments, but on the other hand there is a danger that 'I may on the whole be maile a much less valuable inikiaire than it was at Cm begin- ning. 'rho (Tiverninent I Ito HOW definitely decided not togivu the agriculture labourers any benefits under the Bill, but a compromise him been arrang.al with regard to Cio building ii aloe. This compromiw is a curiosity in legislation. As the BM w.m originally drafted all men employed is building Cry WI were excludeil from any claim to compeimation for injuriei, but an alminilinent which has been accepted by the Home ficcrilury</p>
        <p>provides tliu Co naati:n shad begin whorl a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-06-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>building is raised more than thirty feet from the ground, and in connection with which scaffolding or machinery is need. This seems to mean that if a workman is maimed or killed when the scaffolding ii, say, twenty-nine foot from the ground he or his relatives will not be entitled to any compensation, but , after the struc- ture is thirteen inches higher, compensation will follow an accident as a matter if course. This is what is culled legislation by compromise; it cer- tainly has no logical imeits about it. Eve. 801110 Of thO Goverunnsit's own supporters urged that buildings from the ground upwards should be in- cliniod,bot tho condirzt we of tho Bill were obdur- ate, awl this illogical awl rather ridiculous compromise has now received Parliamentnry en- dorsomont. lu regard tel the miners the Govern- ment have stuck to their Bill manfully, in spite of the bitterest opposition of the coal-owners. Some of these gentlemen have gene so for as almost to predict the ruin of the coal trade because the 11111 will, thoy say, add twoponce or perhaps twopence- halfpenny to the oral, of cool. It must be ad- mitted that the public generally has shown no a"gns of alarm. l'wopence a ton dias not impress them as a very serious :natter. That and a good Ira! mere besides has sometimes been added to the price if coal whoa there has been no counter veiling advantages in the shape oh nomponnation for injured miners. Itesiilm that, Mr. CllANflOglt. LAIN bad no diftimilty on Wedneaday in demolish- ing the arguments on which the conl-owners' cwie hae been built. and he /argued with a goml deal of force that tho which the new Bill will throw upon 7,11.1- owners will not amount to more than three-farthings per ton. Yet it is said that the coal-owners nem will he ro-fought in the House of Lords with a ;raater promise of success than in the Holm of Commons.</p>
