        <p>There are few people who will not heartily en- dorse the protest made by Councillor W. Pierpoint at the Police Court last Monday against the trial of one defection. in private whilst others are dealt with putiliely.The particular instance which called forth t.lll' protest seems to have been an exception- .-- - ally Bagrant one. There were two charges of furious driving to come before the Court on Monday, bat it was found that one of the defendanta had heeti dealt with privately half-an-hour before the regular Court opened at ten o'clock. It is rather startling to lino that such an irregular proceeding as this could be carried out under any circutn- 'dances. People nOW naturally want to know Low it, is dime. Mr. therpoint was tohl by the Mayor that ho--that is, Mr. Pierpoint —was not head boon' there. Well, we take it there ate no bead boas." in connection with the administration of Justice, unless the collective body of magistrates may be described as such. But we do not believe that the magintratea sea hotly have ever even con- sidered, hit Alontl authorised, any such nnistr and iniudici•l methods of dealing with car'. brought before them by the police. how, then, wax thin vary private Ilearitlß arranged in thie instance? Canons or two magistrates who may be—let us say, indiscreet—form a court and dimpose of any particular..., in oontra t If that be so, it is time some steps were taken either by the matmstraten themnelves or by Rouse other responsible authority to put an evil to the eyst em,or there may he • grave ecandal some of these days.</p>
